You have a group of participants who will talk

18
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:58,800
about that.

19
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,280
But in the reading for today, and I know for the first time

20
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,640
we've done this, so you may not have read it, but if you

21
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:07,440
have, that'd be great, is another thread of the kind of

22
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,840
way that people are studied, which is dramatic

23
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:13,480
individual stories, case studies, single stories that

24
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,560
are illustrative of something that we think are

25
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:16,880
broader principles.

26
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:18,800
And a lot of our biggest discoveries, and we'll come

27
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:21,000
to that later in the course, have been made on very

28
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,880
unusual single individuals who turn out to have a lesson

29
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:26,880
that's pretty broad, for example, for the

30
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,680
organization, the human brain.

31
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:32,760
So in the Oliver Sacks book, there's a story about Donald.

32
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:38,680
And what did Donald do that got him in big trouble?

33
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:41,760
He murdered his girlfriend while he was on PCP.

34
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:45,520
He's in a psychiatric hospital.

35
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:47,520
They try hypnosis to help him remember it.

36
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:49,400
He says he doesn't remember it.

37
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,080
Many of you may know that an astounding number of

38
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:55,800
criminals under interrogation tell you they don't

39
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:59,440
remember anything about the episode involved.

40
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:02,000
So people are suspicious at first, for many reasons,

41
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,720
because the easiest thing to say is I don't remember what

42
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,520
happened, and then you can't catch yourself in a lie or

43
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:10,200
any complexity, because you don't remember what happened.

44
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:12,400
And sometimes you don't remember, and sometimes you do.

45
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:14,520
But in this case, they try hypnosis.

46
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:15,200
That doesn't help.

47
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:17,880
He's in there for four years.

48
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:22,160
And then he knows conceptually that he murdered his

49
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:25,320
girlfriend, and he says, I'm not fit for society.

50
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,400
He knows it's a terrible thing he did.

51
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:29,440
But it's kind of like an intellectual knowledge, right?

52
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:30,400
It's not a feeling.

53
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,600
It's not a memory.

54
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:37,760
And then he goes biking and something happens.

55
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:42,160
Which is, he gets hit by a car.

56
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:46,600
And so there's what appears to be a drug-induced amnesia of

57
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:48,720
the original murderous episode.

58
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:50,080
He gets hit by a car.

59
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:51,440
He has a severe head injury.

60
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:53,120
He's in a coma for two weeks.

61
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,480
He has left-sided weakness as he comes out of it, which

62
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:59,800
means the biggest injury was in the right side of his brain.

63
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:00,800
Weakness and numbness.

64
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,400
And he has frontal lobe contusions.

65
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:04,840
He has injury and bleeding around the frontal lobes,

66
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:07,360
the cortex that sits in the front of the brain.

67
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:09,040
But now a remarkable thing happens.

68
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:13,480
It's as if two insults, two wrongs made a right.

69
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:16,520
The first wrong being the PCP, the second wrong being the

70
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,160
accident, and now what happens?

71
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:23,000
He remembers in vivid and florid detail the horror that he

72
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,480
actually committed this murder.

73
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:27,680
It's as if two insults to the brain, one drug-induced, one

74
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,760
a focal injury, repressed the memory, and

75
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:32,360
then unrepressed the memory.

76
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:34,280
And one of the first things I have to do is discover whether

77
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:36,560
he really has his memory or he just thinks he has his

78
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:37,120
memory.

79
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,640
And how do they figure out to their satisfaction whether it's

80
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:44,280
a really recovered memory or a fabrication of his mind?

81
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:48,320
Right, that's really important.

82
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,160
He remembers details of the murder that are not publicly

83
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:54,000
available, that were not in the information that was given

84
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:54,360
to him.

85
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:56,560
That's a really huge issue in these kinds of memories.

86
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:58,600
So there's a really recovered memory, not an

87
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:00,760
inadvertently fabricated memory.

88
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:04,160
And they discover he has deep seizure activity in his limb

89
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,160
structures, the parts of his brain that are involved in

90
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:10,280
emotion are having seizures.

91
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:13,600
In epilepsy, neurons are firing for no good reason, and

92
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:14,560
that's part of his disorder.

93
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:17,440
They give him medications to help with that.

94
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:20,200
But here's the question for you for a moment to think about.

95
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:22,880
And it's now such a common question because our society

96
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,040
is moving that way, that it's on many TV shows.

97
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:26,080
I see it every couple of weeks.

98
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,400
There's a Law and Order episode that has something like

99
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:32,240
this, which is the more you know about the biology that

100
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:36,760
underlies really criminal, terrible behavior.

101
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:40,640
Should that be a cause for understanding it, or not?

102
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:43,400
So in this case, he took the drugs, he killed the person,

103
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,480
you think he's pretty responsible.

104
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:47,880
But how about somebody who kills somebody, and it turns

105
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:50,080
out, in such a case, a man with no history, a grown man

106
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:54,320
with no history of criminal activity of any kind, shoved

107
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:58,160
his wife out of a high window to her death in the course of

108
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,960
a standard argument.

109
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:05,200
Then they discovered that he has a big tumor growing in the

110
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:07,480
basal part of his frontal cortex.

111
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:11,920
Now, is the man responsible for what he did?

112
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:18,760
It's a really interesting question, right?

113
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:21,080
And the more we understand about the neuroscience of our

114
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:23,520
brains, what makes us do things which lets us control

115
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:26,680
impulses, what makes us murderous, there's a guy, Kent

116
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,040
Keele, University of Mexico, who's driving around in a big

117
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:32,520
van with an MRI scanner, and he goes from penitentiary to

118
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:35,840
penitentiary, and he images the brains of serial

119
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:38,360
murderers, of psychopathic murderers.

120
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:40,280
And he's getting things that are pretty systematically

121
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:42,560
different about their brains.

122
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,320
And the question is, what does that mean?

123
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:46,160
Does that mean those people were prone to do it for

124
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:48,440
reasons of their early environment, or genes, or

125
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:50,040
some incident that happened?

126
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:51,960
If that's the case, do you forgive them differently?

127
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:55,400
Should we have a different moral code, or legal code as we

128
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:57,760
discovered the brain basis for some of these things?

129
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:01,080
Or is that irrelevant, what counts as the action?

130
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:03,280
So this is the kind of thing you could think about, maybe

131
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,520
discuss in your section some.

132
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,600
The more we understand about the human brain, the more we'll

133
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:09,360
understand about behaviors that we find terrible.

134
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:12,200
And what does that mean about legal and moral decisions about

135
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:14,520
